  "rationale": "The fleet facts show that pipelined is the only family that can meet the constraints: 54 feasible designs out of 73 evaluations, and it owns all 13 designs on the merged front. The best feasible area is 39973 um^2. The other families (iterative, unrolled_k, pipelined_m) have 0 feasible designs, so they cannot meet the throughput or system constraints. I am focusing 100% of the remaining budget on pipelined, narrowing the parameter ranges around the known-feasible region to refine the Pareto front. The key trade-off is between area (minimized by smaller data_width and n_iter) and accuracy_bits (maximized by larger n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, and rounding). The accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) sets a lower bound on n_iter and data_width, while the area objective pushes toward the minimum feasible values. The throughput constraint (>=32 Msps) requires Fmax >= 32 MHz, which pipelined can meet with reasonable n_iter. The system constraint (sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5) requires that all 32 results are back within 0.5 us of the tick, which for a pipelined design with 1 result/cycle means the pipeline latency plus the time to process 32 requests must be <= 0.5 us. At Fmax = 32 MHz, 1 cycle = 31.25 ns, so 32 requests take 32 cycles = 1000 ns = 1 us, which exceeds 0.5 us. This means the design must run at a higher Fmax or have a lower latency. For a pipelined design with N iterations, the latency is N+2 cycles. If Fmax = 64 MHz, 1 cycle = 15.625 ns, and 32 requests take 32 cycles = 500 ns = 0.5 us, which just meets the constraint. So the design must have Fmax >= 64 MHz to meet the system constraint. This means n_iter must be small enough that the critical path allows Fmax >= 64 MHz. I am narrowing n_iter to 12-16 to focus on designs that can meet both the accuracy and system constraints while minimizing area."
}

